  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The point $P$ is located in the third quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{2}{5}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $P?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\frac{4}{25})+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $y$ is negative in the third quadrant. So our final answer is $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$",
